A modifier key on its own does not show the focus.

Every key event a window receives switches on its focus-visible state,
which is how GTK tells keyboard use from pointer use: from then on the
widget with the focus is drawn with its focus ring.  That included a
modifier pressed and let go on its own, which moves nothing -- above all
the Alt that starts Alt+Tab.  The switcher only learns that it is wanted
when Tab comes, so the press of Alt has already gone to the focused window,
and every window Alt+Tab left showed a ring round its focused button (in
LikeOS Explorer the new-tab button in the title row) when it was switched
back to.  Alt, Shift, Control and Super alone now leave the state as it
is; any other key switches it on as before, and Alt still shows the
mnemonics.

